In Java, initializing a semaphore with zero permits (new Semaphore(0)) means that the semaphore starts in a fully locked state.
No thread can immediately pass through it.

Here is exactly how it behaves and why you would use it:
 The Core Behavior
 ==================
    Immediate Blocking:
    The very first thread that calls semaphore.acquire() will block and wait.
    It cannot proceed because there are zero permits to give away.

    Unlocking Mechanism:
    The semaphore will only open up when a different thread calls semaphore.release().
    Calling release() increases the permit count from 0 to 1, waking up a waiting thread.

 Why Initialize with 0?
 ======================
    While a semaphore with 1 or more permits is used to protect a limited resource, a zero-permit semaphore is used to coordinate execution order between threads.
    It forces one thread to wait until another thread signals that it is ready.